[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation"]
[StudyName "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Repertoire 1: \"Open\""]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/CfSYkcMp/5TBLqn1J"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Jisu101"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2p5/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.01.30"]
[UTCTime "10:44:49"]

2. d4 { In the Open repertoire, we will play d4, taking hold of the center. Black has to respond to this by playing d5. } { [%cal Gd7d5] } 2... d5 { [%cal Ge4d5] } 3. exd5 { We take. Obviously black takes back with the pawn. } 3... cxd5 4. c3 { This marks the beginning of the Exchange Variation of the Caro-Kann. There are a few different setups black can go for. He can play the g6 setup, he can play e6-Bd6, but there is one universal truth in the Caro-Kann Exchange Variation: the bishop goes to f5. Often black waits to show his cards and plays a waiting move like Nf6 or Nc6.
GENERAL PLANS:
We will play Bd3, Bf4, Nf3, and castle kingside. The other knight usually goes to d2, though sometimes it can also go a3-c2. } { [%cal Gc8f5,Gf1d3,Gc1f4,Gg1f3,Gb1d2] } 4... Bf5 (4... Bf5) (4... Nc6) (4... Nf6) *


[Event "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire: Section 1: 4...Bf5"]
[Date "2024.01.30"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Section 1: 4...Bf5"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/CfSYkcMp/DtQHkJaj"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Jisu101"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/8/3p4/3P4/2P5/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.01.30"]
[UTCTime "10:44:49"]

4... Bf5 { Black immediately develops. This chapter will be a foundation for the rest of Repertoire 1, so I decided to put this chapter before the more popular moves. } 5. Bd3 { Not the best move, since Qb3 is better. However, it is very practical, since it exchanges off the bishop on f5, which is one of black's main strengths in the Caro-Kann.
Here it will be black's turn to show his cards. There are 2 different moves here: Bxd3 and Bg6. } { [%cal Gd1b3] } 5... Bxd3 { To my surprise, the best and most played move. } (5... Bg6 { A move very typical of e4-e5 positions, but in the Caro-Kann it is less accurate. } 6. Bxg6! { The best move! Normally this is good for black, but here it is more weakening than that it is good. } 6... hxg6 7. Nf3 { Our plan is very simple. We just castle kingside, play Bf4, our knight goes to d2, and we can play h3 to restrict black's rook. A very nice square for our knight could be e5, with the possibility to sacrifice on g6 or f7 later. } { [%csl Gf4,Gg1,Re5,Rg6,Rf7,Bh3][%cal Gc1f4,Ge1g1,Rf3e5,Re5g6,Re5f7,Gb1d2,Bh2h3] }) 6. Qxd3 { Our general plans are the same in almost all positions. We just play Nf3, Bf4, sometimes there are aggressive ideas with Qb5. The position is approximately equal, but white is definitely freer to do what he wants to do. } { [%csl Gf4,Gf3,Rb5,Rb7][%cal Gc1f4,Gg1f3,Rd3b5,Rb5b7] } *


[Event "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire: Section 2: 4...Nc6"]
[Date "2024.01.30"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B13"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Exchange Variation, Rubinstein Variation"]
[StudyName "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Section 2: 4...Nc6"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/CfSYkcMp/rlr462uK"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Jisu101"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/8/3p4/3P4/2P5/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.01.30"]
[UTCTime "10:44:49"]

4... Nc6 { A simple and useful waiting move, just developing material. We will do the same.
The downside of waiting like this is that we can prevent Bf5. } 5. Bd3! { A very effective move. Bf5 is now not possible for black, and f5 is the only good square. Black's best try in these kinds of positions is to either keep the bishop on c8 and activate it in the middle- or endgame, or playing Bg4 and exchanging it off for the knight that will go to f3. } { [%csl Gf3,Gc8][%cal Gd3f5,Gg1f3,Gc8g4,Gg4f3,Rc8f5] } 5... Nf6 6. Bf4! { We wait with playing Nf3! Otherwise black could play Bg4 and exchange material. Black is a little bit tied up after this move. } 6... Bg4 { The most popular try to activate the bishop. } (6... e6 { With the idea of keeping the bishop on c8. A downside of this move, however, is that the rook cannot come to c8 in the middlegame. } { [%csl Gc8] } 7. Nd2 $14 { We just continue to develop normally. We play Ngf3, castle short, and Bb5 and Ne5 follow in the middlegame. } { [%csl Gf3,Gg1,Bb5,Be5][%cal Gg1f3,Ge1g1,Bd3b5,Bf3e5] }) (6... g6 { Playing with a different plan - Black wants to play Bf5, but puts the other bishop on g7. However, there's a big downside. I can just chop the bishop off the board and enter the middlegame with a far superior pawn structure and black's kingside being weakened a lot. } { [%cal Gc8f5,Gf8g7] } 7. Nf3 Bg7 8. O-O O-O 9. Nbd2 Bg4 (9... Bf5?! 10. Bxf5 gxf5 11. Ne5! { We have a far superior position. Just as I said, the kingside is very weak. There are a lot of attacking ideas for white here. } { [%csl Rf5,Rf7][%cal Gg3g8,Gf1e1,Ge1e3,Ge3g3] }) 10. h3 { We have no problems. }) 7. Qb3! { Black's in some trouble now. We just develop with tempo while the black pieces can't seem to find any good squares! Next up, we play Nd2 and then Ngf3. } { [%csl Rb7,Gd2][%cal Rb3b7,Gb1d2,Gg1f3] } *


