4... Nf6 5. Bd3 { Again, the same as last chapter, except for one extra possibility for black. } { [%cal Rd3f5,Rc8f5] } 5... Bg4 (5... Nc6 { We have seen this exact position in Section 2 of this Repertoire 1 (Chapter 4). }) 6. Qb3! { Remember? :) } 6... Qc7 { However, it is slightly different. The black queen has access to c7 because our bishop is not yet on f4. But now we cannot play Bf4, because the Queen is on c7. } { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rc7f4] } 7. h3! { And where can the bishop go? } 7... Bh5 (7... Bd7 8. Nf3 { We can very simply develop our pieces. Our bishop goes to g5 instead of f4, but that's ok. } { [%csl Gg1,Gg5][%cal Ge1g1,Gc1g5] }) 8. g4! { Very aggressive! } 8... Bg6 9. Bxg6 hxg6 10. g5! { Winning the pawn on d5. However, the position remains to be very sharp. Black definitely has a bit of counterplay, so be ready to sometimes have to defend a little bit. } { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rg5f6] } *


[Event "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire: Repertoire 2: \"Half-Open\""]
