[UTCTime "10:44:49"]

2. Nf3 { We enter Repertoire 2. We are not intending to play d4 right away, but first let black exchange on e4 and then play d4. } 2... d5 3. Nc3 { The idea is to take back on e4, and then play d4. Black's plans are very similar - Bf5 and Bd6, Nf6, Nbd7.
We will look at 4 different moves from black in this repertoire.
GENERAL PLANS:
We would love to play Ng3 and kick the black bishop away. We will also play d4, Bd3, Bf4, and sometimes c3. h4-h5 is a very common pattern. } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Gc3e4,Ge4g3,Rg3f5,Gd2d4,Gc2c3,Gc1f4] } 3... dxe4 (3... dxe4) (3... Bg4) (3... d4) (3... Nf6) *


[Event "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire: Section 1: 3...dxe4"]
[Date "2024.01.30"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Crush the Caro-Kann! White Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "Section 1: 3...dxe4"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/CfSYkcMp/mluuzWsD"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Jisu101"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pp2pppp/2p5/3p4/4P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 1 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.01.30"]
[UTCTime "10:44:49"]

3... dxe4 { By far the most common move. Black enters the Mainline of the Two Knights Attack. Of course, there is the crazy 4.Ng5!!? but I'm not going to cover it since we will keep it simple. So, we take on e4. } 4. Nxe4 Bf5 (4... Nf6 { This is actually the most common move. Black just wants to simplify and gain some center himself. } 5. Nxf6+ { We exchange. } 5... exf6 6. d4 { We have a nice center and a small lead in development. Our bishop usually cannot go to f4, but that's fine. Ideally, we keep the bishop on c1 to come into action in the middlegame. So, we play Bd3, castle kingside, and solidify the center with c3. } { [%cal Gf1d3,Ge1g1,Gc2c3] }) (4... Bg4 5. h3! { Simple. } 5... Bh5 (5... Bxf3 6. Qxf3 { We have a good position. We play Bc4, d3, and Be3/f4. } { [%csl Gc4,Gd3,Ge3,Gf4][%cal Gf1c4,Gd2d3,Gc1f4,Gc1e3] }) 6. Ng3 Bg6? (6... Bxf3 7. Qxf3 { Again, we just play simple moves and get a good position. }) 7. h4! { This position is also in the mainline, so check out that. } { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rh4h5] }) (4... Nd7 5. d4 { [%csl Gd4] } 5... Ngf6 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. c3 { We have a nice lead in development and a good center. } { [%csl Gd3,Gf4][%cal Gf1d3,Gc1f4] }) 5. Ng3! { Kicking the bishop. 5...Bg4 is the only good move, but for 88% of people, it's over now. } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rg3f5] } 5... Bg6?? 6. h4! { Black has 2 choices to save his bishop - h6 and h5. } { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rh4h5] } 6... h6 { 6...h5 fails, but as we will see, this move also fails to save the game. } (6... h5 { Blocking the pawn fails. } 7. Ne5! { If Bh7, the pawn on h5 hangs. } { [%csl Rg6,Rh5][%cal Re5g6,Rd1h5] } 7... Qd6 { [%cal Rd6g6] } 8. d4 $16 { We are dominating. }) 7. Ne5 { The queen infiltrates with decisive effect. } { [%csl Rg6,Rg4,Rh5][%cal Re5g6,Rd1g4,Rd1h5] } 7... Bh7?? { This allows us to basically get into a won position. } (7... Qd6 8. d4 { [%csl Re5][%cal Rd4e5] } 8... Nd7 { The only way to try to save the game. } 9. Nxg6 Qxg6 10. c3! { Black's queen is misplaced, the kingside pieces are still on its starting squares - we can just solidify our position. } { [%csl Rd4,Rf8,Rg8,Rh8,Rg6][%cal Rc3d4] }) 8. Qh5!! { We simply go for f7. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rh5f7] } 8... g6 (8... Qd5 { An interesting defensive try, but it fails. } 9. Bc4! { We're crushing. We win at least a pawn, with a winning position. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7] }) 9. Bc4! { We sac our queen for mate! Obviously, e6 is forced. } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rc4f7] } 9... e6 10. Qe2! { We have a big threat of Nxf7! Black's bishop is locked in, almost all his pieces are on his back rank, while we are just attacking already. Let me show you how the attack works: } { [%csl Rf7,Rh7,Rh8,Rg8,Rf8,Re8,Rd8,Rb8,Ra8][%cal Re5f7] } 10... Nf6 (10... Qe7 { Best, but we are far better anyways. } 11. d4 $18) 11. Nxf7!! $18 { There are too many threats to deal with! We win massive amounts of material or mate. } { [%csl Rd8,Rh8,Re6][%cal Rf7d8,Rf7h8,Re2e6] } 11... Kxf7 12. Qxe6+ Kg7 13. Qf7# *


