3... Bg4 { The Mindeno Variation. This move is played about 25% of the time and is not very easy to refute, as there is a lot of theory. However, we will get an advantage. } 4. h3 { [%csl Rg4][%cal Rh3g4] } 4... Bxf3 { The mainline. } (4... Bh5 5. exd5 cxd5 6. g4! Bg6 7. d4 { We are doing great. } { [%csl Rh5,Re5][%cal Rh3h4,Rh4h5,Rf3e5] }) 5. Qxf3 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rf3d5,Rc3d5] } 5... e6 { Reinforcing the pawn. Other moves are also possible. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Ge6d5] } (5... Nf6 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rf6d5] } 6. d3! (6. e5? { Don't do this! } 6... Nfd7 7. d4 e6 { Black has a surprisingly solid position which is almost impossible to break through. }) 6... e6 7. Be2 { We keep the tension in the center. Black has equality, but the position can take turns fast. } { [%csl Gd5,Ge4][%cal Ge4d5,Gd5e4] }) (5... dxe4 6. Nxe4 { Once again, there's a split path. } 6... e6 (6... Nf6 7. Nxf6+! exf6 8. c3! { Preparing d4. Now our bishop does go to c4. } { [%csl Rd4,Gc4][%cal Rd2d4,Gf1c4] }) (6... Nd7 7. Qe2 { Surprisingly, the trap works pretty well as a normal move too. } 7... e6 { Of course black avoids it. } (7... Ngf6?? { The well-known trap... } 8. Nd6#! { [%csl Re8][%cal Rd6e8] }) 8. g3! { We develop our bishop on g2. The other bishop goes to f4. } { [%cal Gf1g2,Ge1g1,Gd2d4,Gc1f4] }) 7. d3 { The bishop won't do much on c4, so we can go to e2. } { [%csl Ge2,Gf4][%cal Gf1e2,Gc1f4] }) (5... d4?! { This doesn't work that well because of a surprising refutation: } 6. Bc4! { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rf3f7] } 6... e6 (6... dxc3?? { I will include this to show the attack: } 7. Qxf7+ Kd7 8. dxc3 { There is absurd compensation for the piece. We have two pawns, an unstoppable attack starting with Bf4, and much more. } { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rc1f4] }) (6... Nf6? 7. e5! dxc3 8. exf6 { Material is equal, but our position is far better. }) 7. Ne2 c5 8. Qb3 { There can also be potential sacrifices on e6. We are better. } { [%csl Rb7,Be6][%cal Rb3b7,Bb3e6] }) 6. Be2 { Once again, some different moves black can play. } { [%csl Gd5,Ge4,Gg1][%cal Ge4d5,Gd5e4,Ge1g1] } 6... Nf6 (6... Nd7 7. d4 { We finally play d4. Next, we play exd5 and Bf4. } { [%csl Gf4,Gd5][%cal Gc1f4,Ge4d5] }) 7. O-O { [%csl Ge4,Gd5][%cal Ge4d5,Gd5e4] } 7... Be7 (7... Nbd7 8. d4 { We simply play d4. }) (7... dxe4 8. Nxe4 Nxe4 9. Qxe4 Nd7 10. b3! { A very surprising move, with a 65% winrate! We just play d4, Bb2, and get our rooks ready for the attack. } { [%csl Gb2,Gd4,Gd1,Ge1][%cal Gc1b2,Gd2d4,Ga1d1,Gf1e1] }) (7... Bb4? 8. e5! { We advance. } 8... Nfd7 9. d4 $14) 8. d3 { We develop our bishop on the normal squares. It's a positional game, both sides have chances. } { [%csl Rf4,Rg5][%cal Rc1f4,Rc1g5] } *


