[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.01.30"]
[UTCTime "10:44:49"]

3... Nf6 { Black plays aggressive immediately. We respond in Alekhine-style. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rf6e4] } 4. e5 Ne4 (4... Nfd7 5. e6! fxe6 6. Ng5 { Black has to be very careful here. } { [%csl Re6,Rh5][%cal Rg5e6,Rd1h5] }) 5. Ne2! { Surprise! We don't want to exchange knights (yet). } 5... Qb6 { [%csl Rf2][%cal Rb6f2] } (5... Bg4 6. Nfg1!! { Threatening a simple fork. This is one of the craziest lines of opening theory I've ever seen. We just move everything backwards! } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rf2f3,Gb1c3,Gc3e2] } 6... Bxe2 (6... Bh5 7. f3 Nc5 8. d4 { Our simple plan is to play Nf4/g3 to exchange the bishop off. } { [%csl Rf4,Rg3][%cal Re2f4,Re2g3] }) (6... Qb6 { [%csl Rf2][%cal Rb6f2] } 7. d4 Bxe2 8. Nxe2 { We simply play f3 to chase away the knight. } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rf2f3] }) 7. Nxe2 e6 8. d4 { We are definitely doing great! }) 6. d4 e6 (6... Bg4 7. Nfg1! { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rf2f3] } 7... Bxe2 8. Nxe2 { Simple. } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rf2f3] }) 7. Ng3 { The best move. } 7... c5 (7... Nxg3 8. fxg3! c5 9. Bd3! { transposes back to the mainline. }) 8. Bd3! { Very simple, threatening to win a pawn. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd3e4,Rg3e4] } 8... Nxg3 9. fxg3! cxd4 10. O-O { Our bishop stands solid in blocking the pawn. Once we've played Kh1, we can move the bishop and win the pawn back. } { [%csl Rd3] } 10... Nc6 11. a3! { Again, absolutely necessary! The knight wants to go to b4 and exchange our bishop, which would not be fantastic for us. We have a good advantage. } { [%csl Rb4,Rd3][%cal Rc6b4,Rb4d3] } *


