2. c4 { Entering Repertoire 3. The main idea is to force an IQP position and play aggressively. } 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. cxd5 { Here we enter the splitup. Nf6 is the best move, getting back into normal Caro-Kann theory. But Qxd5 is the most common move online, and the game starts to look more like an Alapin Sicilian with 2...d5 (1.e4 c5 2.c3 d5 3.exd5 Qxd5 4.d4 cxd4 5.cxd4, which is the same position as 1.e4 c6 2.c4 d5 3.exd5 cxd5 4.cxd5 Qxd5 5.d4, which we will look at in this study).
GENERAL PLANS:
We want to play d4, plant a knight on e5 and attack on the kingside with your pieces.
WARNING: This repertoire requires a lot of study and practice to play well. You need to use a lot of your time just studying and playing games. In the beginning, you will lose quite some games. I recommend playing some casual games 'From Position' with your friends. But once you get better at this and start to remember all the lines, your rating will start to increase. Because this repertoire has a lot of lines to study, I only recommend it to 1800+ players or players with more than enough time. } { [%csl Gd4,Re5,Gc3][%cal Gd2d4,Rg1f3,Rf3e5,Gb1c3] } 4... Nf6 (4... Nf6) (4... Qxd5) *


