4... Qxd5 { Most common online, the game now transposes into an Alapin Sicilian. } 5. d4 Nc6 { By far the most common move, attacking the d4-pawn. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd5d4] } (5... Nf6 6. Nc3 { We will simply develop. Black does not have an ideal position. The plans are similar to Scandinavian structures. I've shown the normal moves with arrows. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Gf1c4,Gc1e3,Gg1f3,Rf3e5,Gc1f4] }) (5... e5 6. Nf3 { Simply developing and defending the pawn. } 6... exd4 (6... e4? 7. Nc3! Bb4 { Is forced to not lose a pawn. } 8. Ne5 Nc6 9. Nxc6 { We're doing great! }) 7. Qxd4) 6. Nf3 { Defending the pawn and preparing to chase away the queen. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Gf3d4,Rb1c3] } 6... Bg4 { Again, by far the most common move you'll see. } (6... e5 7. Nc3 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc3d5] } 7... Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 9. Bxc3 e4 10. Ne5 Nxe5 11. dxe5 { Simple. Black's knight cannot go to f6, and the queen is undefended. So, 2 choices. After Qxd1 we obviously take back with the rook. } { [%csl Rd1,Rf6,Ge7][%cal Ra1d1,Re5f6,Rg8f6,Gg8e7,Ge7d5,Gd5d1] } 11... Ne7 12. Be2 { We don't trade, as that would help black. Instead we castle, and then play Rc1. } { [%csl Gg1,Gc1][%cal Ge1g1,Ga1c1,Rc1c8] }) (6... Nf6?? 7. Nc3! Qd8 (7... Qa5 8. d5 Nb4 9. a3 Nbxd5 10. Bb5+! Bd7 11. Bxd7+ Kxd7 12. b4 $18 { Forcing the queen away and we win the knight. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc3d5,Rd1d5] }) 8. d5 Nb8 (8... Nb4?? 9. Qa4+ { Picks up the knight. } { [%csl Rb4][%cal Ra4b4] }) 9. Bc4 $18 { Our position is crushing. } { [%csl Ra8,Rb8,Rc8,Rd8,Re8,Rf8,Rh8,Gd5][%cal Ge1g1,Gc1f4] }) (6... e6 7. Nc3 { [%cal Rc3d5] } 7... Bb4 (7... Qd8 8. Bd3 Nf6 9. O-O Be7 10. Re1 { We will just play simple moves and we'll have a good middlegame. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Gc1f4,Rf3e5] }) (7... Qa5 8. Bb5 Bd7 (8... Bb4 { [%csl Rc3,Rb5][%cal Rb4c3,Ra5b5] } 9. O-O Bxc3 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. bxc3 Qxc3 12. Qa4!! { No explanation needed. The arrows tell everything. } { [%csl Rc6,Ra8,Re8][%cal Ra4c6,Rc6a8,Rc6e8] } 12... Bd7 13. Ba3 { We are crushing. Down a pawn, but more than enough compensation. } { [%csl Rf8][%cal Ra3f8] } 13... Nf6 14. Rac1! Qd3 15. Rxc6 Bxc6?? 16. Qxc6+ Kd8 17. Qxa8+ Kc7 18. Qxh8 Qxa3 19. Qxg7 { 1-0. It's basically over. }) 9. O-O { We're doing great. }) 8. Bd3 Nf6 9. O-O { We once again threaten to take the queen. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc3d5] } 9... Bxc3 10. bxc3 O-O 11. Re1 { We're planting our knight on e5, and we have a far superior position. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Rf3e5] }) 7. Nc3! { We can attack the queen anyways. } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rc3d5] } 7... Bxf3 { Forced, but played only 33% of the time!! We can easily crush other moves. } (7... Qa5?? 8. d5! { And now everything loses: } 8... Ne5 (8... O-O-O?? 9. Qb3 { Here we need to play slightly differently. } 9... Bxf3 10. gxf3 Nd4?? (10... Ne5 11. Be3 Nxf3+?? 12. Ke2 { [%csl Rc8,Rh4,Rg5][%cal Rb3c4,Rc4c8,Rc4h4,Re3g5] } 12... Ne5 13. Nb5! { It's basically over here. } { [%csl Rc1,Rf4,Rb8,Rc8][%cal Ra1c1,Re3f4,Rf4b8,Rc1c8] }) 11. Qc4+ { We win the knight. } { [%csl Rd4,Rc8][%cal Rc4d4,Rc4c8] }) (8... Rd8?? 9. Bd2 { Now we threaten to take the knight again. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rd5c6] } 9... Nd4 (9... Ne5?? 10. Nxe5!! Bxd1 11. Bb5+ Rd7 (11... Qxb5 12. Nxb5 { It's mate or the loss of a piece. } { [%csl Rd1,Rc7][%cal Ra1d1,Rb5c7] }) 12. Bxd7+ Kd8 13. Rxd1 { We have a big attack, and should be able to win even more material. } { [%csl Rf7,Rc8][%cal Re5f7,Rd1c1,Rc1c8] }) 10. Nb5!! { Winning on the spot! The queen is attacked, the white queen comes to a4 with a deadly discovered check, Nc7 is also gameover, it's just too much. White wins a lot of material. } { [%csl Ra5,Ra4,Rc7][%cal Rd2a5,Rd1a4,Rb5c7] } 10... Bxf3 (10... Qb6 11. Qa4!! { A deadly threat of Nc7#. } { [%csl Re8,Rc7][%cal Ra4e8,Rb5c7] } 11... Nxf3+ (11... Nxb5 12. Bxb5+ Bd7 13. Bxd7+ Rxd7 14. Rc1 { There's too much. Rc8, Ba5, Ne5, it's simply over. } { [%csl Rc8,Ra5,Re5][%cal Rc1c8,Rd2a5,Rf3e5] }) 12. gxf3 Bd7 { Forced to try to defend. } 13. Be3! { Black loses his queen because it has to defend c7. 1-0. } { [%csl Rc7][%cal Rb5c7] }) 11. gxf3 { Simple. The same threats are still there. } { [%csl Ra5,Ra4,Rc7][%cal Rd2a5,Rd1a4,Rb5c7] }) 9. Qb3!! { Now it's too much to handle. } { [%csl Rb7,Re5][%cal Rb3b7,Rf3e5] } 9... Nxf3+ (9... Bxf3? 10. Qxb7! { Wins on the spot. } 10... Rd8 (10... Bxd5 11. Bb5+ Kd8 12. Qxd5+ { And it's over now. Feel free to try to find the mate in 8. }) 11. Bb5+ Nd7 12. gxf3 { A pawn up, having a good attack, and more. Black can resign if he wants to. }) 10. gxf3 { There's too much. We win the b7-pawn, we can get a strong attack with Bb5, and black's bishop is hanging at the moment. } { [%csl Rb7,Rg4,Rb5][%cal Rb3b7,Rf3g4,Rf1b5] }) (7... Qd8?? 8. d5! { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rd5c6] } 8... Ne5 9. Nxe5! Bxd1 10. Bb5+ Qd7 11. Bxd7+ Kd8 12. Kxd1 f6 13. Nf7+ Kxd7 14. Nxh8 { The awkward placement of the queen left us up a rook. }) 8. Nxd5 Bxd1 9. Nc7+ Kd7 10. Nxa8 { The position is 0.0 (!!!) according to the computer, but it's absolutely a mess. We have to play Bf4 to free our knight. At the same time, our pawn is hanging with the potential threat of Nc2+, our knight is stuck in the corner (for now at least), black's pieces on the kingside are not yet developed, and white is up an exchange.
Absolute madness.
I will continue this line in the next chapters, as black can respond in a few different ways. } { [%csl Rd1,Rc2,Rf8,Rg8,Rh8][%cal Re1d1,Rc6d4,Rd4c2,Rc1f4,Ra8c7,Gf4c7] } *


